WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.970
When Disney announced they were remaking their
much-loved classic animation Beauty And The

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:09.140
Beast, fans wondered what exactly the new
filmmakers would keep or change and whether

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.410
the new movie could ever reach the incredibly
high bar set by the original.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.810
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I want to take a look at

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.470
some of the very best and some of my least
favourite changes and differences between

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.470
Disney's live-action Beauty And The Beast
and the 1991 animated movie.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.500
Naturally, I'll be delving deep into spoiler
territory, so be aware of that if you haven't

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.790
seen the new movie yet.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:34.160
If that's you, you can check out my spoiler-free
review of the film by tapping or clicking

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.160
here.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.380
I loved how they took Belle's character from
the original film and expanded and updated

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.039
it in what felt, to me, like a very natural
way.

00:00:41.039 --> 00:00:45.480
So, Belle's still a literature-loving young
woman as she was in 1991, but she also wants

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.480
to pass that love and knowledge on to other
young girls in her town.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.390
And not only does she help her father with
his own craft, making music boxes, like she

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.319
did with his inventions in the animation;
but she's also an inventor in her own right,

00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:59.800
creating contraptions to make life easier
and give her more time for the things she

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.390
enjoys, like reading and sharing her knowledge.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:06.530
We also see Belle firmly shutting Gaston out
of her house and plotting her escape from

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.299
the Beast's castle, and even her clothes reflect
the fact she's an active character, for example,

00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:15.750
she wears boots and her skirt's hitched up
on one side to make riding her horse easier.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.420
The updates to Belle's character are not only
fitting given the fact this is a film for

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.470
modern audiences, but they're also a continuation
of what began back in 1991 with Linda Woolverton,

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.440
who was the first female screenwriter of a
Disney animated feature.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.119
Woolverton wanted Belle to be less passive,
more independent, and more relevant to contemporary

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:37.250
audiences than previous Disney princesses,
but often found it an uphill struggle to get

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.500
her ideas through to the final film, for example,
a scene Woolverton wrote with Belle marking

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.280
a map with all the places she wanted to visit
was switched to show Belle baking in the kitchen

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.200
until it was agreed that Belle would walk
around reading instead.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.189
Much as I love the original animation, I think
the new filmmakers made a wise decision when

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:58.609
they decided not to have Gaston jump the gun
and set up a surprise wedding for Belle!

00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:02.759
Gaston's still super-full of himself in the
new film, but what we get instead of him putting

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:06.899
his feet up at Belle's house and assuming
she's going to marry him, is Gaston trying

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:11.120
a subtler but perhaps more devious approach
by claiming to sympathise with Belle over

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.430
her mistreatment by the headmaster who put
a stop to her using her new washing invention.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.550
It's still clear from their conversation that
they'd be hopelessly mismatched as a couple,

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:23.680
and there are several nice nods to the scene
from 1991, including Gaston alluding to Belle

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.580
having his children and, also, after she rebuffs
him, he walks off and steps into a muddy puddle,

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.620
a call-back to the animation where he lands
head-first in a muddy pond after Belle shows

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:33.760
him the door.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.230
Gaston has many great moments in the animated
film, and Luke Evans does a fabulous job bringing

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.849
him to live-action life, giving his character
a more grounded feel than the original by

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:45.489
playing him so real and the comedy so deadpan.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:50.780
One of the many things I love about the new
movie is Maestro Cadenza and Madame de Garderobe.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.810
Obviously, Cadenza is a new addition to the
castle's characters, but I love how he fits

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.010
in well with the existing characters thanks
to the fact they make Garderobe his wife,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.690
and make her human counterpart an Italian
opera diva.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.810
Oh, and making their dog Froufou become the
Footstool character from the original movie

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.930
was also a really nice touch.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.780
Also, their character design and the way that's
carried through into their human forms is

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:12.930
just wonderful.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.330
Garderobe, especially, is a triumph of imagination.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.049
In her wardrobe form, inside her doors, you
see stage curtains, footlights, and box seats,

00:03:21.049 --> 00:03:24.860
all of which really bring through the fact
that, as a human being, she's a performer

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.790
and also that she has a rather dramatic, theatrical
personality.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.360
And it's such a neat detail that after the
enchanted Cadenza has fought the angry mob

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.770
by launching his harpsichord keys at them
as if they were bullets, when he's turned

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:39.300
back into a human being at the end, he's missing
many of his teeth!

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:43.610
Audra MacDonald and Stanley Tucci, who bring
Garderobe and Cadenza to life, are just brilliant

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.190
in the film, both in their own right and together.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:50.110
I love how fun their characters are, I love
the sweet way they interact with each other

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:54.360
in the film, I love how various easter eggs
and hidden details are introduced into the

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.411
film through their characters, I love the
very talented actors who play them, and I

00:03:58.411 --> 00:04:01.799
love the fact their relationship adds to the
movie's diversity.

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:05.549
Speaking of which, Garderobe's character is
also part of another scene that adds another

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:09.910
element of diversity to the movie, when in
the final battle, she wraps three men in dresses

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:14.150
– two of whom run off, as in the animated
film, while the third is delighted to have

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.120
come out of the closet, so to speak.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.690
Of course, there's been a lot of talk about
LeFou being gay in this film as well, with

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.660
his character experiencing mixed feelings
for Gaston and dancing briefly with a man

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:24.950
during the final celebration.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:30.330
It's actually all very subtle, and certainly
no reason to raise the rating, cut scenes

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.100
or refuse to screen it, which is what's happened
in some parts of the world.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:38.491
The film's already updating the tale for modern
audiences in terms of Belle's character and

00:04:38.491 --> 00:04:43.080
making the cast more racially diverse, and
excluding LGBT characters from that diversity

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.460
would quite simply in my opinion be wrong.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.962
One of the lovely touches that the filmmakers
have introduced into this movie is that as

00:04:49.962 --> 00:04:54.169
time passes and each petal falls from the
Beast's rose, we see and hear the effects

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:56.510
of this on the castle and the enchanted servants.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:01.780
So, Lumiere and Cogsworth's joints start to
seize up, making it harder for them to move,

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:06.620
the duster Plumette gets several new feathers,
and the wardrobe Garderobe becomes increasingly

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:11.230
sleepy and lethargic to the point where she
falls asleep after only semi-finishing a crazily

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.090
over-the-top dress for Belle.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:17.430
And on several occasions, the castle itself
rumbles and creaks as various bits collapse

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.170
and fall away from its exterior.

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:23.460
Showing the castle and its inhabitants reacting
to the effects of the spell over time is a

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:27.430
beautiful and subtle addition to the movie's
world, and is perhaps a nod to how in the

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.580
Broadway musical, the effect of the spell
on the household servants shows itself gradually.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.660
Anyway, I loved how it integrated so well
into the story and design of the movie, and

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:40.490
how at the end as the last petal falls, the
servants became inanimate, and later resume

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.360
their human forms in delightful ways, for
example, the duster Plumette emerges from

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.380
a pile of feathers.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:50.680
Of the new songs written by composer Alan
Menken and lyricist Tim Rice for the live-action

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.970
film, my favourite is Evermore, which the
Beast sings after he sets Belle free to go

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:56.400
and help her father.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.931
It's a really gorgeous musical number full
of loss, longing, and sacrifice and actor

00:06:00.931 --> 00:06:03.360
Dan Stevens performs it so well.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.169
And Days In The Sun, which I already liked
the first time I saw the film, really got

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:08.789
me the second time I watched the movie.

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:13.340
It's such a wonderfully melancholic lullaby-like
song sung by the enchanted servants about

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.789
their former lives and it's really very moving.

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:20.190
It'll be interesting to see if any of the
new songs get Oscar nominations next year.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:24.669
Oh, and it's not a new song, and she just
gets a few lines, but I love hearing Audra

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:27.620
Macdonald as Garderobe sing Beauty And The
Beast right at the end.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:31.449
She's got such a beautiful voice and presence
that it seems unbelievable that years ago

00:06:31.449 --> 00:06:34.610
when she auditioned to be in the ensemble
for the Broadway musical of Beauty And The

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.569
Beast, she didn't get the job!

00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:41.060
Anyway, she totally nails the song here – she's
just fantastic – and add to that the new

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:45.360
Beauty And The Beast lyrics that Emma Thompson
sings as Mrs Potts at the end, and it's just

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.479
perfect!

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:49.720
By the way, if you want to know more about
those new lyrics and how they came about,

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.500
I talk about that in my Easter Eggs video,
which you can click or tap here to watch.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.639
One line I love in the animation that doesn't
make it into the live-action film, which I

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.919
think is a real shame, is 'Gaston, you are
positively primeval', which Belle says in

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:07.930
response to Gaston's comments about how women
shouldn't read as it'll give them ideas and

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:12.430
how she should be paying attention to more
important things than books, namely him.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:16.780
What we get instead of that and Belle's refusal
when Gaston invites her to 'look at his trophies'

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:21.240
in the tavern, is a scene where Gaston tries
to invite himself to dinner with Belle, which

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.210
she turns down, only making the uber-arrogant
Captain even more keen.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:28.830
While I did enjoy this new scene, I still
would have loved to have heard that line from

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.030
Belle in the new film.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:34.690
As soon as they announced that Frozen star
Josh Gad was going to play LeFou, one image

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:37.170
from the animated film popped straight into
my mind.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:41.349
Yes, it was the scene where LeFou, who's been
forced by Gaston to wait for Belle and Maurice

00:07:41.349 --> 00:07:44.810
outside their house, gets covered in snow
like a snowman.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:49.330
Obviously, Josh Gad's character in Frozen
is the snowman Olaf, so I was really hoping

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:54.150
they'd recreate that snowman scene from the
1991 movie, even though I knew it would be

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:58.860
such an obvious joke and that alone would
mean they probably wouldn't do it!

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.190
In the end, because of the way they've changed
the story in the new film, the events that

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:06.330
lead up to that snowman scene in the animated
movie wouldn't make sense here, but I would

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.900
still have loved to have seen Josh Gad in
live-action snowman form!

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:16.290
But despite the fact I didn't get my Snow-Gad
in the movie, I think Josh Gad is superb in

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.770
Beauty And The Beast and works so well together
with Luke Evans as Gaston.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.409
One moment in the film that didn't seem as
natural or smoothly executed as the rest to

00:08:23.409 --> 00:08:25.280
me was when Belle meets Lumiere.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.540
In the original film, Lumiere introduces himself
to her before the big song-and-dance number,

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:30.540
Be Our Guest.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.400
But in this film, she meets him earlier when
he goes to her cell and offers to escort her

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.400
to her room.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:39.030
Belle seems a bit more taken aback in this
version when she meets the enchanted servants

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:42.500
for the first time, but that's not my issue
with the change, as it'd be perfectly normal

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.850
for her to be shocked to see a talking candelabra
for the first time.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:49.790
What didn't work for me in this new scene
was the way her reaction to him seems delayed

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:50.790
or a bit disjointed.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:55.100
What I mean is that she's already seen him
and talked to him, but she still decides to

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.540
go ahead and hit him with a stool and then
after that she also screams.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:01.860
I can see what they were trying to do with
the scene, and how they were also echoing

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:06.420
Maurice's reaction to Lumiere from the 1991
film, but the way the scene's been put together

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:11.340
here is incoherent, as if it wasn't edited
or scripted quite right, so it ended up not

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.010
ringing true for me.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:16.810
When I first saw the trailers for Beauty And
The Beast, I've gotta say I wasn't super-keen

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.730
on the look of the enchanted objects, especially
Mrs Potts.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:24.610
I mean, I just love how expressive she looks
in the animated version, and in the new film,

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:29.000
she seems less so, and on top of that, of
course, the animated Mrs Potts has a spout

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.470
for a nose, whereas the spout on the live-action
Mrs Potts is there as a sort of arm, which

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.630
may have been inspired by her design in the
Broadway musical.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:40.090
When did I see the full live-action film for
the first time, I've gotta admit that the

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:44.930
production design did win me over as the rather
ornate and refined design of the objects fits

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:48.470
well with the mid-17th-century time setting
they've chosen for the film.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:52.491
Still, as much as I love Emma Thompson's rendition
of 'Tale As Old As Time' and her lively take

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:56.920
on Mrs Potts, that role will still always
belong to Angela Lansbury for me.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.080
As for Ewan McGregor as Lumiere, while I did
enjoy the overall performance, especially

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.000
the 'Be Our Guest' extravaganza, it does sound
like he had a tough time with the French accent,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.800
and he's actually said as much himself.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:12.300
So, again, much as I love Lumiere and the
new movie as a whole, it's still Jerry Orbach's

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:14.670
animated Lumiere that wins the day for me.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:18.700
And I'd say the same for Nathan Mack's take
on Chip in the live-action movie versus Bradley

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:20.470
Pierce's animated Chip as well.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:24.850
Now, I'd love to hear your verdict about the
changes between the original animation and

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.610
Disney's live-action remake.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.100
What did you most enjoy and what didn't you
like?

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.120
Let me know in the comments below.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:34.160
And while you're here, why not be my guest
and enjoy some of my other Beauty And The

